{
 "format": "mechanism-workshop",
 "version": 1,
 "id": "example-slidercrank",
 "name": "Belt-driven slider-crank",
 "description": "A motor drives a small pulley; an (ideal, non-slipping, inextensible) belt turns the 3.5× larger crank pulley; a pin on that pulley drives a connecting rod and a slider in a slotted guide. A constant 0.8 N process force and a little guide friction act on the slider.\n\nStroke = 2 × the crank pin radius of the large pulley. Change it (or a pulley diameter, the rod length, the motor speed) and the change propagates: pulley geometry → pin position → rod and slider pose → belt routing and ratio → plots.\n\nThe belt is a kinematic coupling on the pitch circles (ratio = pitch diameters); its drawn path is the tangent-line routing of the same two circles and its pitch length is reported in the BOM. Belt stretch, slip and pre-tension are not modelled.\n\nThe last variant makes the stroke longer than the guide: the slider runs into the end of the slot and the interference checker flags the pair with the time and overlap.",
 "createdAt": "2026-01-01T00:00:00.000Z",
 "modifiedAt": "2026-01-01T00:00:00.000Z",
